.\" elc.1 — manual page for elc.
.\"
.\" This page describes the version it ships with (HLR-129). Every option elc
.\" accepts appears here, and every option here is accepted; the documentation
.\" test in test/integration/docs.bats enforces both directions.
.TH ELC 1 "2026-08-18" "elocker" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
elc \- per-function code metrics and whole-project architecture analysis
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B elc
.RI [ OPTION ]...
.IR TARGET ...
.br
.B elc
.B \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B elc
reports code metrics per function, and analyses the architecture that connects
them, for a codebase in any language it has runtime support for.
.PP
A
.I TARGET
is a source file or a directory. Several may be given in any combination; a
file reached through more than one target is analysed once.
.SS Current state
.B elc
is under active development. This release discovers the files in its targets,
parses each one, and reports effective lines of code and cyclomatic
complexity per function, per file, and per language. The System Dependence
Graph and the findings derived from it are delivered by later phases, and
this page grows with them. See
.B doc/SDP.md
in the distribution for what each phase adds.
.SS Effective lines of code
.B ELOC
is the count of lines carrying an executable statement: an assignment or
operation, a control-flow construct, a call, a return, or exception handling.
It excludes what serves only a structural, declarative, blank, or documentary
purpose — a blank line, a line holding nothing but a brace, a declaration
that initialises nothing, a preprocessor directive, and a comment.
.PP
Two properties follow from counting
.I lines that carry statements
rather than counting either physical lines or statements:
.TP
.B Layout does not move the number.
A statement spread over four lines counts once, at the line it starts on, so
reformatting a long condition changes nothing. Two statements written on one
line likewise count once — that line is one line to read.
.TP
.B A nested function's work belongs to it.
Where a language allows a named function inside another,
.B elc
reports both, and each statement counts for the innermost function containing
it and for no other. An enclosing function is not credited with what its
nested functions do.
.PP
Comments are identified from the syntax tree, never by matching text. A
comment opener inside a string literal opens nothing, a quote inside a comment
opens no string, and a comment sharing a line with code does not stop that
line counting.
.SS Cyclomatic complexity
A function's cyclomatic complexity is
.B one plus
the number of decision points in it: the number of paths through it. A
function that never branches is 1.
.PP
A decision point is a place execution can go more than one way — an
.BR if ,
a loop, a
.B case
label, a conditional expression, and each short-circuiting
.B &&
or
.BR || ,
since each of those is a second place the condition can be decided. Three
things that look like decisions are not: a bare
.B else
adds no path the
.B if
did not already count, a
.B default
label is where control goes when no branch was taken, and a
.B goto
moves control without choosing.
.PP
As with effective lines of code, a nested named function owns its own
decision points and the function enclosing it gains none of them. A decision
point inside an
.I anonymous
callable — a lambda or closure, which is not reported as a function in its own
right — is attributed to the nearest enclosing named function.
.SS The complexity threshold
.B elc
lists, for each file, the functions whose complexity is at or above a
threshold, set with
.B \-\-complexity\-threshold
and defaulting to 15.
.PP
The threshold changes
.I what is listed
and nothing else. It does not change a total, a callout, or — most
importantly — the exit status. Findings are data; deciding what a number
warrants is the caller's. A build that should fail on a breach acts on the
report rather than expecting
.B elc
to exit non-zero.
.SS Language support
.B elc
determines each file's language from its extension, and loads the parser and
queries for that language from the runtime directory at first use. Nothing
language-specific is compiled into the executable: adding a language means
adding files under
.IR runtime/ ,
never rebuilding. The interface a language module must satisfy is published
as
.I runtime/queries/README.md
in the distribution.
.PP
A file whose extension maps to no available language is
.I skipped
rather than failed: it is named on standard error, listed in the report's
skipped-file section, and does not affect the exit status. A language whose
module is unusable — absent, exposing no entry point, or carrying a query
file that will not compile — is reported once, excluded, and the run
continues over the remaining languages. Only a runtime directory that yields
no language at all is fatal.
.SS Discovery
A target that is a regular file is analysed directly, with no traversal. A
target that is a directory is walked recursively. During that walk
.B elc
excludes hidden files and hidden directories, and files whose extension
appears in
.IR runtime/binary.exts .
.PP
A symbolic link encountered during the walk is not followed, so a directory
reached through a link is never descended into and a self-referential link
cannot produce an unbounded traversal. A symbolic link named
.I directly
as a target is resolved and analysed, since naming it identifies its referent.
.PP
Every target is validated before any of them is walked, so an invalid target
is reported whatever else was named and no report is produced that covers
fewer targets than were given. Each discovered file is reduced to its
canonical absolute path and analysed exactly once, however many targets reach
it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-complexity\-threshold " " \fIN\fR
List a file's functions whose cyclomatic complexity is
.I N
or greater. The default is 15. Listing only: no threshold affects the exit
status.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIFILE\fR
Write the report to
.I FILE
instead of to standard output. Diagnostics continue to go to standard error,
so the file holds results and nothing else. The default is standard output.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display a usage summary on standard output and exit successfully. Requesting
help is not an error, so the exit status is 0 rather than a failure code.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The three classes are distinct so that a caller can tell a degraded run from a
run that never happened. No finding severity ever contributes to the exit
status: findings are data, and acting on them is the caller's decision.
.TP
.B 0
Every discovered file was processed without error, or skipped because no
language module was available for it. A skipped file is not a failure.
.TP
.B 1
The run completed and produced a report, but at least one discovered file
failed to be read or parsed. The report covers the files that succeeded.
.TP
.B 2
The run did not complete and no report was produced: a usage error, an
invalid target, a fatal runtime-location failure, or a rejected saved record.
.SH OUTPUT
Results are written to standard output, or to the file named with
.BR \-\-output ;
diagnostics are written to standard error. Nothing else is written to the
results stream, so output may be piped or captured without being corrupted by
diagnostic text.
.PP
The default format is an aligned table in seven sections: the project
summary, the project-wide callouts, the totals broken down by language, one
row per analysed file, one row per function, the functions at or over the
complexity threshold, and the files that were skipped. Column widths are taken from the longest value in each column so the
figures line up:
.PP
.EX
Project summary
  Files             2
  Physical lines   42
  ELOC             18
  Functions         3
  Skipped           1

Callouts
  What          Value  Where
  ------------  -----  ---------------------------------
  Largest file     12  /home/u/proj/src/a.c
  Most complex      7  parse in /home/u/proj/src/a.c

Languages
  Language  Files  Lines  ELOC
  --------  -----  -----  ----
  c             2     42    18

Files
  File                  Language  Lines  ELOC  Functions
  --------------------  --------  -----  ----  ---------
  /home/u/proj/src/a.c  c            18    12          2
  /home/u/proj/src/b.c  c            24     6          1

Functions
  File                  Function    Lines  ELOC  Complexity
  --------------------  ----------  -----  ----  ----------
  /home/u/proj/src/a.c  parse        5-19     9           7
  /home/u/proj/src/a.c  emit        21-24     3           1
  /home/u/proj/src/b.c  main         3-11     6           2

At or over the complexity threshold (5)
  File                  Function  Complexity
  --------------------  --------  ----------
  /home/u/proj/src/a.c  parse              7

Skipped files (no language module)
  /home/u/proj/src/notes.md
.EE
.PP
A function's line range runs from its signature to the end of its body. A
named function declared inside another is reported in its own right rather
than folded into the one containing it.
.PP
A file's ELOC may exceed the sum of its functions': a statement outside every
function — an initialised global, say — counts for the file and for no
function. It may also fall below that sum, where two functions share a line.
.PP
Every section is emitted whether or not it has rows, so the report has the
same shape whatever the target held. Files are listed by canonical absolute
path in ascending byte order, and functions within a file in start-line
order. That
order is a property of the report rather than of the filesystem, so two runs
over the same tree produce byte-identical output, and so do two runs that
name the same targets in a different order.
.PP
A run in which no file was found still produces this report, with zero totals
and an empty table, and exits successfully. Silence would be
indistinguishable from a failure.
.PP
The shape of the report does not vary with the type of the target: a single
file, a plain directory, and a repository all produce the same headings and
the same columns.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ELC_RUNTIME_DIR
Location of the runtime directory holding language grammars and their query
files. When set, it takes precedence over the
.B runtime
directory adjacent to the executable.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I runtime/parsers/<lang>.so
Tree-sitter grammar for a language, loaded on first use.
.TP
.I runtime/queries/<lang>/
The query files that define what elc extracts for that language.
.TP
.I runtime/extensions.map
Maps a file extension to a language name, one pair per line. Editing this
file changes what is detected; no rebuild is needed.
.TP
.I runtime/queries/README.md
The published contract a language module must satisfy: the query files it
supplies and the capture names by which they return their results.
.TP
.I runtime/binary.exts
Extensions excluded from the directory walk, one per line. Editing this file
changes what is excluded; no rebuild is needed.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.B elc
has none. Behaviour is determined entirely by the command line and the
contents of the runtime directory. No configuration file or dotfile is read
or discovered, from the working directory, the analysis target, or any
ancestor of either, so two users running the same command on the same tree
obtain the same result.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B elc \-\-help
Show the usage summary.
.TP
.B elc src/
Analyse a directory.
.TP
.B elc src/main.c src/cli.c
Analyse named files.
.TP
.B elc src/main.c src/
Analyse a file and a directory that contains it. The file is reported once.
.TP
.B elc \-o report.txt src/
Write the report to a file, leaving standard output empty.
.TP
.B elc \-c 10 src/
List every function whose complexity is 10 or greater. The exit status is
unchanged by what the list contains.
.TP
.B ELC_RUNTIME_DIR=/opt/elc/runtime elc src/
Analyse with language support taken from a runtime directory elsewhere.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cloc (1),
.BR gprof (1)
.PP
